# Local styx-emulator patch wiring up the MPC866M (MPC860 / PowerQUICC I) event
# controller, whose `insert_exception`, `latch`, `execute` and `tick` are
# `todo!()` upstream, so any MPC860 run that completes an instruction stride
# panics and hangs.
#
# Cut against styx-emulator 71a7746fe192a56b257549842cc3ec55ffc8f75a
# (the `rev` pinned in ../default.nix). The two modules it declares - `event`
# and `exception` - are installed from ../mpc860/ by `postUnpack`.
#
# This controller registers no code hook of its own. `rfi` has to be
# intercepted (Unicorn would run it against QEMU's own SRR0/SRR1, which nothing
# populates), but the PowerQUICC SIU already owns a whole-range per-instruction
# hook, and `CodeHook::call` is not told which address it was dispatched for -
# so a second hook would have to find its instruction via `pc()`, which the
# `rfi` path rewrites. Both hooks cannot coexist; see `mtspr_proxy` in
# ./powerquicci-mtspr.patch, which owns the opcode and calls back into
# `return_state` here.
#
# The SRRs are modelled as one shadow pair, not a stack: the hardware has one,
# and both the guest (`mtspr SRR0/SRR1`, mirrored in by that same hook) and
# exception entry write it. A stack cannot tell "return from exception" from
# "`rfi` used as an atomic jump", so a handler that leaves by any other route
# strands a frame that then hijacks the next unrelated `rfi`.
#
# Touches no Cargo.toml, so the vendored crate set and `cargoDeps` hash are
# unchanged; keep it that way or both hashes in ../default.nix must be redone.
#
# Remove once styx-emulator implements this controller upstream.
